Says, at debug, what each frame drawn after an idle spell spent between
being asked for and reaching the screen: when its redraw was asked, how
long its render waited to begin and took, and how long it waited for
the screen once queued, with the refreshes from the ask. A shell opening
its launcher after a quiet moment saw its frame reach the screen a
median 6 refreshes after its swap at 120 Hz, though niri queues a
redraw as a layer surface commits and renders it at once. The times are
by the monotonic clock presentation feedback uses, so a client's own
timings line up with niri's line. An output counts as idle once 100 ms
have passed since its last frame reached the screen, so a steady stream
of frames says nothing. It adds no setting; the tty backend alone says
it, since only it knows when a frame reaches the screen.

